VERDICT
Correct: your code uses a nested loop to check every index pair (i, j) where i is less than j and accurately counts all matching pairs.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You initialized count to 0 and returned it properly at the end.

You set up the nested loops so that j always starts at i + 1, ensuring i is strictly less than j and preventing duplicate pairs from being counted in reverse.

Your condition nums[i] == nums[j] directly matches the definition of a good pair.

Is it correct?

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There are no logical bugs under LeetCode constraints where nums.length is between 1 and 100, and elements are between 1 and 100.

Minor signed/unsigned type warning: nums.size() returns an unsigned type (size_t), while i and j are signed int.

Subtle edge case in C++: if nums is empty, nums.size() - 1 wraps around to a massive positive number due to unsigned integer underflow, causing an out-of-bounds crash. The problem guarantees nums has at least 1 element, so it will not crash on LeetCode.

Edge cases handled properly: array with all identical elements (for example [1, 1, 1, 1], which returns 6), array with all distinct elements (for example [1, 2, 3], which returns 0), and an array with only one element.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(n^2) where n is the length of nums, because the nested loops check roughly n * (n - 1) / 2 pairs.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space, because only two loop variables and one counter are stored in memory.

Why my approach works:

Take nums = [1, 2, 3, 1, 1, 3]. When i = 0 (value 1), j scans forward and finds matches at index 3 (value 1) and index 4 (value 1), adding 2 to count. When i = 2 (value 3), j finds a match at index 5 (value 3), adding 1 to count. When i = 3 (value 1), j finds a match at index 4 (value 1), adding 1 to count. The total count returned is 4.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Brute Force Nested Loops (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

LeetCode official editorial Approach 1.

Idea:

Use two loops: for every element at index i, look at every element after it at index j. If nums[i] is equal to nums[j], add 1 to your counter.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numIdenticalPairs(vector<int>& nums) {
        int count = 0;
        int n = nums.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (nums[i] == nums[j]) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n^2) because it compares every pair of elements.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because it uses only simple counter variables.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of checking all pairs across the array, sorting the numbers places identical numbers next to each other so we can count repetitions in a single pass.

Approach 2: Sorting and Combinatorics

Where it comes from:

Classic sorting technique found in LeetCode discussion forums and GeeksforGeeks.

Idea:

Sort the array first. Identical elements now form contiguous groups. For each group of k identical numbers, the number of good pairs formed among them is k * (k - 1) / 2.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numIdenticalPairs(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        int count = 0;
        int streak = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {
                streak++;
            } else {
                count += streak * (streak - 1) / 2;
                streak = 1;
            }
        }
        count += streak * (streak - 1) / 2;
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n log n) due to sorting the array of size n.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space (or O(log n) stack space for IntroSort).

What makes the next approach better:

Sorting takes O(n log n) and modifies the array. By using a hash map or frequency table, we can count how often each number occurs in O(n) linear time without sorting.

Approach 3: Hash Map Frequency Counting with Math Formula

Where it comes from:

LeetCode official editorial Approach 2 and standard counting dictionary patterns.

Idea:

Count the frequency of every unique number in an unordered_map. For each number that appears k times, add k * (k - 1) / 2 to the total count.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numIdenticalPairs(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> freq;
        for (int num : nums) {
            freq[num]++;
        }
        int count = 0;
        for (auto const& [num, k] : freq) {
            count += k * (k - 1) / 2;
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) average time to populate the hash map and iterate through unique elements.

Space Complexity:

O(u) where u is the number of unique elements (at most n) stored in the hash map.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of counting frequencies in one pass and computing the formula in a second pass, we can accumulate pairs on the fly as we read the array.

Approach 4: Single-Pass Hash Map (Running Pair Accumulation)

Where it comes from:

Top-voted LeetCode community solutions.

Idea:

Imagine people entering a room with numbers on their shirts. When a new person arrives with number X, they can form a pair with every person already in the room wearing number X. So you add the current count of X to your answer, then increase the count of X by 1.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numIdenticalPairs(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> countMap;
        int ans = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            ans += countMap[num];
            countMap[num]++;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) average time because it makes one pass over the array with O(1) hash map operations.

Space Complexity:

O(u) where u is the count of distinct elements in nums.

What makes the next approach better:

An unordered_map involves hashing overhead and possible collisions. Since nums[i] is between 1 and 100, a direct fixed array of size 101 gives faster, collision-free O(1) indexing and zero dynamic heap allocation.

Approach 5: Direct Frequency Array (Single-Pass Optimal)

Where it comes from:

LeetCode official editorial and competitive programming constraint-based optimization.

Idea:

Because 1 <= nums[i] <= 100, use a fixed integer array count[101] initialized to zeros. For each number x, add count[x] to the answer, and then increment count[x].

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numIdenticalPairs(vector<int>& nums) {
        int count[101] = {0};
        int ans = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            ans += count[num];
            count[num]++;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because it visits each element in nums exactly once with direct array indexing.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because the array size is fixed at 101 integers regardless of input size.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 1 (Brute Force Nested Loops).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

An interviewer expects Approach 4 (Single-Pass Hash Map) or Approach 5 (Direct Frequency Array). They want to see you recognize that checking identical pairs is a frequency counting problem that drops from O(n^2) down to O(n).

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: Why does ans += count[num] before count[num]++ give the same answer as k * (k - 1) / 2?

Answer: When the 1st copy arrives, it sees 0 earlier copies (+0). The 2nd copy sees 1 earlier copy (+1). The 3rd copy sees 2 earlier copies (+2). For k copies, the running sum adds 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (k - 1), which is mathematically equal to k * (k - 1) / 2.

Question 2: What if nums contains negative numbers or numbers up to 10^9?

Answer: A fixed array of size 101 will no longer work due to out-of-bounds indices and memory limits. You must use Approach 4 (unordered_map<int, int>), which handles arbitrary integer ranges.

Question 3: Can ans overflow a standard 32-bit signed integer?

Answer: For LeetCode constraints (n <= 100), the maximum possible pairs is 100 * 99 / 2 = 4950, which easily fits in int. However, if n can be up to 10^5, the maximum pairs could be roughly 10^10 / 2 = 5 * 10^9, which exceeds 32-bit INT_MAX (2.14 * 10^9). In that case, ans must be declared as long long.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER

The key insight, in one sentence:

Every time you see a number that already appeared k times, it creates k new good pairs with those previous occurrences.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Whenever a problem asks to count pairs of identical items, replace the O(n^2) nested loop with a frequency map or array.

Accumulate pairs on the fly (ans += count[x]; count[x]++;) instead of doing two separate loops.

Check the range of nums[i]: if values are small (like 1 to 100), a fixed array is faster than an unordered_map.

Watch out for unsigned size() subtraction (nums.size() - 1) when handling empty vectors.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Frequency Counting and Combinatorial Pair Accumulation (Prefix Counting).

